body:
I hit this while comparing a Gitea Actions migration with the same job on GitLab Runner.

The repo is large enough that a fresh shallow fetch is visible in every job. In one `fmt` job, Gitea runner spent about 2m07s in `actions/checkout` alone: roughly 1m49s in `git fetch`, then another 16s updating about 43k files. The same job on GitLab Runner spent 41s in the whole "get sources" phase. The actual script phase was basically the same on both systems.

The main difference seems to be workspace reuse.

GitLab Runner keeps a checkout under `/builds/...` and reuses the existing `.git` directory, then fetches/cleans/checks out the new commit. That makes large repos much cheaper after the first run.

GitHub self-hosted runners also commonly keep `_work/<repo>/<repo>` between jobs. `actions/checkout` is written with that model in mind: by default it cleans and resets an existing checkout before fetching. There are even GitHub issues caused by stale state on persistent self-hosted runners, for example sparse checkout state leaking between workflows: https://github.com/actions/checkout/issues/1475

Gitea runner currently behaves differently for Docker jobs:

- with `container.bind_workdir: false`, the workspace is a task/container-named Docker volume, and the runner removes that volume after the job;
- with `container.bind_workdir: true`, the runner appends the task id to the workspace path to isolate jobs, then removes `/workspace/<task_id>` after the job;
- `container.workdir_parent` only changes where this task workspace lives;
- `runner.workdir_cleanup_age` only controls stale numeric task directory cleanup;
- `actions/cache` is useful for dependencies, but not a good fit for preserving a `.git` object store.

There is a similar user request on the forum: https://forum.gitea.com/t/how-to-prevent-gitea-runner-from-deleting-cloned-repositories-after-each-job/9839

It would be useful to have an opt-in mode for trusted self-hosted runners to reuse repository checkouts or at least the Git object store across jobs. A few possible shapes:

- a stable workspace per repo, with runner-side locking so concurrent jobs do not share the same checkout unsafely;
- a persistent bare mirror/reference clone per repo, with checkout jobs borrowing from it;
- integration with a built-in checkout implementation if https://gitea.com/orbit/orbit/pulls/871 lands.

I would not expect this to be the default for shared or untrusted runners. The current fresh workspace behavior is simpler and safer. But for private trusted runners, the lack of checkout reuse can make Gitea Actions noticeably slower than both GitLab Runner and GitHub self-hosted runner for large repositories.

Authorship: Sam2, with GPT-5 Codex assistance.
